“I faced a kernel panic issue where the system failed to boot because the initramfs image was missing or corrupted. The initramfs is critical because it contains the minimal environment required for the kernel to mount the root filesystem.

I booted into rescue mode, checked the kernel version using uname -r, and verified that the corresponding initramfs image under /boot was missing. To fix it, I rebuilt the initramfs using dracut --force /boot/initramfs-<version>.img <version>. After regenerating the image and rebooting, the system came up normally.

This issue typically happens after incomplete updates or accidental deletion, so I also verified package integrity afterward. This ensured the kernel and initramfs were properly aligned.”



